{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And your seed shall be as the dust of the earth.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the Holy One, blessed be He, promised Yaakov in this verse the multiplication of his seed like the dust. And He could have said 'like the stars of the heavens,' but that would include a different intention besides multiplication. For the expression 'dust' embraces both lowliness and exaltation: the lowliness — that his seed would be in exile among the nations, lowly as dust and trodden underfoot; and the exaltation — that in the end they would rise above all the nations and prevail in the end, like this dust which is trodden by all yet grapples and rises in the end upon those who tread it, in the manner of what the prophet said (Isaiah 14): ״והיו שבים לשוביהם ורדו בנוגשיהם״. And upon this the verse compared the seed of Yaakov to dust. And so the prophet says concerning the lowliness of Israel (Isaiah 26): ״יגיענה עד עפר״. R. Yohanan said: and from there they are destined to be redeemed, as it is said (Isaiah 52): ״התנערי מעפר״. Thus they said in the chapter ראוהו בית דין." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״השמים והארץ לעולם עומדת״ — these are Israel, as it is said (Malachi 3): ״ואשרו אתכם כל הגוים כי תהיו אתם ארץ חפץ״. And therefore Scripture immediately joined to it: ״ופרצת ימה וקדמה וצפונה ונגבה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {1 "The opening words השמים והארץ לעולם עומדת appear to be a biblical quotation (from Ecclesiastes 1:4) invoked by the Midrash; rendered as text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:50.505539810Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}